{"parentUuid":"ee53d48b-878f-4674-b324-180fbe4f93e3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013KCGzguJaintemrefahzdy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"symmetry breaking constraint programming lex-leader Crawford 2024 2025 survey\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2024.3\"},{\"title\":\"Breaking Symmetries with Involutions - DROPS\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.8\"},{\"title\":\"Breaking Symmetries from a Set-Covering Perspective | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-95973-8_11\"},{\"title\":\"General Symmetry Breaking Constraints Toby Walsh1\",\"url\":\"https://cgi.cse.unsw.edu.au/~tw/wcp06.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2407.04419v1\"},{\"title\":\"[2407.04419] The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.04419\"},{\"title\":\"[2407.04419v1] The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.04419v1\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry in Constraint Programming - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1574652606800143\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Symmetry in Constraint Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/240829678_Symmetry_in_Constraint_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found several highly relevant papers on symmetry breaking constraint programming involving lex-leader and Crawford's work:\n\n## Key Papers Found:\n\n**2024 Paper:**\n**\"The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\"** was published in the 30th International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2024). This paper focuses on symmetry breaking predicates (SBPs) that typically impose an order on variables and single out the lexicographic leader (lex-leader) in each orbit of assignments. Although it is NP-hard to find complete lex-leader SBPs, incomplete lex-leader SBPs are widely used in practice, and the paper investigates the complexity of computing complete SBPs.\n\n**2025 Paper:**\n**\"Breaking Symmetries with Involutions\"** was published in the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025). This paper introduces the concept of graph patterns that provide a concise representation for sets of non-canonical graphs (graphs that are not lex-leaders), and four specific graph patterns can identify about 3/4 of all non-canonical graphs.\n\n**Foundation Work:**\nThe notion of symmetry breaking predicates was first introduced in the seminal paper of Crawford, Ginsberg, Luks and Roy, which established the foundational framework still widely used in constraint programming today.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3bd9a0ba-8cd1-44a8-bcbc-b135c6a10451","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:07.590Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ee53d48b-878f-4674-b324-180fbe4f93e3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"026d6e13-ede5-4a11-9f99-a195d4e1a791","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LzdLio35CZXzG8y2H5g9Tw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"OR-Tools CP-SAT symmetry_level parameter detection automorphism\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: Parameter for symmetry finding dtime limit · Issue #4419 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4419\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"SymmetryPropagator | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/reference/sat/symmetry/SymmetryPropagator\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"custom search constraints and symmetry breaking with cp/sat\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/4tqNoPhFnAw\"},{\"title\":\"CDCLSym: Introducing Effective Symmetry Breaking in SAT Solving | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-89960-2_6\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the OR-Tools CP-SAT `symmetry_level` parameter and automorphism detection:\n\n## Symmetry Level Parameter\n\nCP-SAT automatically tries to detect symmetries in a model and exploit them. Currently, at level 1 it detects them in presolve and tries to fix Booleans.\n\nSymmetry finding can be forced on for large problems by setting `symmetry_level:3`, however it will time out after a hard-coded 1 second of deterministic time. The hardcoded time limits are defined at cp_model_symmetries.cc:749 and cp_model_symmetries.cc:936.\n\n## Automorphism Detection\n\nSymmetries of a SAT problem are classically obtained through a reduction to an equivalent graph automorphism problem. The SAT problem is converted to a colored graph, then it is passed to a tool like saucy3 or bliss to compute its automorphism group.\n\nIn the context of OR-Tools, the `SymmetryPropagator` class detects potential literal propagations based on problem symmetries. When a literal is assigned, it checks if a symmetrical literal can also be propagated.\n\nThe search results also indicate that as of December 2025, LRAT proof checking only works with pure SAT problems with cp_model_presolve = false, linearization_level <= 1, and symmetry_level <= 1.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8136d49f-4e16-4c20-87e4-f5ba5a295318","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:08.877Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"026d6e13-ede5-4a11-9f99-a195d4e1a791","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d857a0f1-e95f-41e7-9f35-8d5de0f14618","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VpntxsgVisoxf9VELyhssW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"2D rectangle packing symmetry breaking interchangeable items CP-SAT\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Yet Another Math Programming Consultant: 2d bin packing with Google OR-Tools CP-SAT, how to fix?\",\"url\":\"http://yetanothermathprogrammingconsultant.blogspot.com/2021/02/2d-bin-packing-with-google-or-tools-cp.html\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A SAT-based Method for Solving the Two-dimensional Strip Packing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220445013_A_SAT-based_Method_for_Solving_the_Two-dimensional_Strip_Packing_Problem\"},{\"title\":\"H.: Exact algorithms for the 2-dimensional strip packing problem with and without rotations | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/222703285_H_Exact_algorithms_for_the_2-dimensional_strip_packing_problem_with_and_without_rotations\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04B_advanced_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Symmetry-breaking constraints for packing identical rectangles within polyhedra\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257697346_Symmetry-breaking_constraints_for_packing_identical_rectangles_within_polyhedra\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Solution Techniques for Two-dimensional Cutting and Packing\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0377221720306111\"},{\"title\":\"SciELO Brazil - EFFICIENT SAT AND MAXSAT TECHNIQUES FOR SOLVING THE TWO-DIMENSIONAL STRIP PACKING PROBLEM EFFICIENT SAT AND MAXSAT TECHNIQUES FOR SOLVING THE TWO-DIMENSIONAL STRIP PACKING PROBLEM\",\"url\":\"http://www.scielo.br/j/pope/a/Cz3GM6VVxJRB4XhXFHXW8Br/?lang=en\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Solutions to the Two-Dimensional Bin Packing Problem with Due Dates by Milad Dehghan, Sergey Polyakovskiy, Rym M’Hallah :: SSRN\",\"url\":\"https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/Delivery.cfm/8e0c5a3b-fd9b-4972-aa97-0e55a68a3d12-MECA.pdf?abstractid=5343698&mirid=1\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] A SAT-based Method for Solving the Two-dimensional Strip Packing Problem | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/A-SAT-based-Method-for-Solving-the-Two-dimensional-Soh-Inoue/6c809d4d10924e51deb6a7c40b69f8704b8c85c7\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ktnr/BinPacking2D: Exact solutions for two-dimensional bin packing problems by branch-and-cut · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ktnr/BinPacking2D\"}]\n\nI've found relevant search results on 2D rectangle packing with symmetry breaking and CP-SAT. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview\n\nSAT-based Order Encoding models incorporate item rotation and adapted symmetry-breaking for solving two-dimensional strip packing problems. SAT-based Order Encoding models incorporating item rotation and adapted symmetry-breaking (SB) have been developed to address this challenge.\n\n## Symmetry Breaking Techniques\n\nSymmetry breaking is vital for pruning the search space. While Soh et al. (2010) applied it to fixed-orientation 2SPP, item rotation, a key aspect of many 2SPP variants, necessitates adapted or new techniques. This includes designing new, adapted symmetry-breaking constraints essential for managing the increased combinatorial complexity introduced by rotation.\n\n## CP-SAT Solver Application\n\nGoogle CP-SAT, integrated into Non-Incremental Bisection search framework, performed best overall in comparative studies. CP-SAT offers constraints including add_no_overlap_2d, which is used to prevent overlaps between intervals on two dimensions, making it suitable for rectangle packing problems.\n\n## Recent Research\n\nPerformance of specialized logic-based methods has been benchmarked against leading solvers like Google OR-Tools CP-SAT, providing a clear, contemporary perspective on the competitiveness of SAT/MaxSAT for 2SPP.\n\nThe search results show active research in combining CP-SAT with symmetry-breaking techniques for handling interchangeable (identical) items in 2D rectangle packing problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5075ab2f-b850-45f3-97ad-4b6cb5e8cbeb","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:09.985Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d857a0f1-e95f-41e7-9f35-8d5de0f14618","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f419fce9-43bb-40e1-84cd-ea05f3eed9ec","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KxCzj7kGeKMLQ7FE3XL4TG","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"nauty bliss saucy graph automorphism CP model symmetry detection\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Faster Symmetry Discovery using Sparsity of ... - vlsicad page\",\"url\":\"https://vlsicad.eecs.umich.edu/BK/SAUCY/saucy-dac08.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"saucy (+) and nauty (×) slowdowns on dense graphs. The programs were... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/saucy-and-nauty-slowdowns-on-dense-graphs-The-programs-were-executed-on-the_fig2_2896494\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Graph Symmetry Detection and Canonical Labeling: Differences and Synergies\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/230764359_Graph_Symmetry_Detection_and_Canonical_Labeling_Differences_and_Synergies\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Symmetry Detection and Canonical Labeling: Diﬀerences and Synergies\",\"url\":\"https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~imarkov/pubs/conf/T100-label.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Exploiting Structure in Symmetry Detection for CNF\",\"url\":\"http://vlsicad.eecs.umich.edu/BK/SAUCY/conf/saucy-dac04.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Graph automorphism - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_automorphism\"},{\"title\":\"Practical graph isomorphism, II Brendan D. McKay\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1301.1493\"},{\"title\":\"nauty and Traces: Graph Isomorphism Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://hunterheidenreich.com/notes/interdisciplinary/graph-theory/nauty-traces-graph-isomorphism/\"},{\"title\":\"Hypergraph/design isomorphism as graph isomorphism | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Hypergraph-design-isomorphism-as-graph-isomorphism_fig4_265943088\"},{\"title\":\"nauty and Traces User’s Guide (Version 2.6) Brendan D. McKay∗\",\"url\":\"https://users.cecs.anu.edu.au/~bdm/nauty/nug26.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about nauty, bliss, saucy, graph automorphism, and symmetry detection:\n\n## Overview\n\nSeveral open-source software tools are available for graph automorphism detection, including NAUTY, BLISS and SAUCY.\n\n## Key Differences Between the Tools\n\n**SAUCY:** SAUCY was optimized to only find graph symmetries. SAUCY and BLISS are particularly efficient for sparse graphs, with SAUCY processing some graphs with millions of vertices in mere seconds.\n\n**BLISS and NAUTY:** BLISS and NAUTY can also produce Canonical Labeling, whereas SAUCY is currently optimized for solving Graph Automorphism.\n\n**Performance:** nauty is generally fastest for small graphs and some easier families, while Traces dominates on most difficult graph classes, sometimes by orders of magnitude.\n\n## Algorithms and Techniques\n\nnauty and Traces use individualization-refinement with search tree pruning for graph isomorphism testing and canonical labeling. Competitive algorithms for the graph automorphism problem are based on efficient partition refinement augmented with group-theoretic pruning techniques.\n\n## Symmetry Detection in CP Models\n\nThe automorphism group of a graph is equivalent to the formulation symmetry group, and finding MIP symmetries reduces to finding graph automorphisms; consequently, software such as Nauty or Bliss can be applied to determine generators of the group. Once generators are obtained, orbits—sets of variables which can be permuted without changing the MIP formulation—can be calculated and then exploited in the MIP solver.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0e9ac5ae-8fdc-42aa-8613-8929018a39e8","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:10.739Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f419fce9-43bb-40e1-84cd-ea05f3eed9ec","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
