{"parentUuid":"4700c539-d397-4b99-bf51-120f96a32a79","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012SLjkMoTQNF9MNdbY3wHwR","type":"tool_result","content":"# CP-SAT `symmetry_level` Parameter\n\nThe provided document does not contain information about the `symmetry_level` parameter or its specific values (0, 1, 2, 3, 4).\n\nHowever, the content does mention that CP-SAT performs **automatic symmetry detection** during presolve. The log example shows:\n\n> \"[Symmetry] Symmetry computation done...#generators: 2, average support size: 12...[Symmetry] Found orbitope of size 6 x 2\"\n\nThis demonstrates that CP-SAT automatically identifies interchangeable variables and orbital symmetries without requiring manual configuration.\n\nThe document notes that \"symmetries may be broken by prohibiting equivalent variations of the same solution,\" which can improve performance by reducing search space.\n\n**For specifics on `symmetry_level` values and quantitative speedup data**, you would need to consult:\n- The official `sat_parameters.proto` documentation referenced in the primer\n- OR-Tools source code or additional technical documentation\n- Empirical benchmarking on your specific problem instances\n\nThe primer focuses on beginner-friendly parameters and explicitly cautions against adjusting advanced settings without understanding their effects."}]},"uuid":"73081f24-515f-4999-ba81-dcaf8464a672","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:21.650Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4700c539-d397-4b99-bf51-120f96a32a79","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
