{"parentUuid":"a9ccc37e-7458-4f75-bce0-a5718f89093c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015MFFMuDeevY2FgzNhdu29s","type":"tool_result","content":"4540\t        for tpl, vars_by_pose in self.optional_pose_vars.items():\n4541\t            if tpl not in self._powered_templates or tpl == \"power_pole\":\n4542\t                continue\n4543\t            pose_coverers = self._power_coverers_by_template_pose.get(tpl, {})\n4544\t            for pose_idx, z_var in vars_by_pose.items():\n4545\t                coverers = pose_coverers.get(pose_idx, [])\n4546\t                if not coverers:\n4547\t                    self.model.Add(z_var == 0)\n4548\t                    constraints += 1\n4549\t                    continue\n4550\t                self.model.Add(sum(pole_vars[idx] for idx in coverers) >= z_var)\n4551\t                constraints += 1\n4552\t\n4553\t        self.build_stats[\"power_coverage\"] = {\n4554\t            \"constraints\": constraints,\n4555\t            \"pole_cap\": None if self.exact_mode else self._optional_cap_by_template.get(\"power_pole\", 0),\n4556\t        }\n4557\t\n4558\t    def _add_symmetry_breaking_constraints(self) -> None:\n4559\t        # Grouped encoding（分组编码） already removes clone permutations（克隆置换）.\n4560\t        self.build_stats[\"symmetry_breaking\"] = {\"grouped_encoding_only\": True}\n4561\t\n4562\t    def _ordered_groups_for_exact_search(self) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n4563\t        if not self.exact_mode:\n4564\t            return list(self._mandatory_groups)\n4565\t\n4566\t        candidate_counts: Dict[str, int] = {}\n4567\t        for group in self._mandatory_groups:\n4568\t            group_id = str(group[\"group_id\"])\n4569\t            candidate_counts[group_id] = len(self._candidate_pose_indices_for_group(group))\n4570\t\n4571\t        return sorted(\n4572\t            self._mandatory_groups,\n4573\t            key=lambda group: (\n4574\t                int(candidate_counts.get(str(group[\"group_id\"]), 0)),\n4575\t                str(group[\"facility_type\"]),\n4576\t                str(group[\"group_id\"]),\n4577\t            ),\n4578\t        )\n4579\t\n4580\t    def _ordered_optional_pose_indices(self, tpl: str) -> List[int]:\n4581\t        return sorted(\n4582\t            self.optional_pose_vars.get(tpl, {}),\n4583\t            key=lambda pose_idx: self._pose_sort_key(tpl, int(pose_idx)),\n4584\t        )\n4585\t\n4586\t    def _ordered_ghost_anchor_indices(self) -> List[int]:\n4587\t        return sorted(\n4588\t            self.u_vars,\n4589\t            key=lambda rect_idx: (\n4590\t                int(self._ghost_domains[int(rect_idx)][\"anchor\"][\"x\"]),\n4591\t                int(self._ghost_domains[int(rect_idx)][\"anchor\"][\"y\"]),\n4592\t                int(rect_idx),\n4593\t            ),\n4594\t        )\n4595\t\n4596\t    def _add_search_guidance(self) -> None:\n4597\t        if not self.exact_mode:\n4598\t            self.build_stats[\"search_guidance\"] = {\n4599\t                \"applied\": False,\n4600\t                \"profile\": \"default_automatic\",\n4601\t                \"reason\": \"exact-guided branching only runs in certified_exact mode\",\n4602\t            }\n4603\t            return\n4604\t\n4605\t        mandatory_literals = 0\n4606\t        ghost_literals = 0\n4607\t        optional_literals: Dict[str, int] = {}\n4608\t        required_optional_literals: Dict[str, int] = {}\n4609\t        residual_optional_literals: Dict[str, int] = {}\n4610\t        mandatory_signature_counts: Dict[str, int] = {}\n4611\t        required_optional_signature_counts: Dict[str, int] = {}\n4612\t        mandatory_signature_count_literals = 0\n4613\t        required_optional_signature_count_literals = 0\n4614\t        required_optional_templates = [\n4615\t            tpl\n4616\t            for tpl in sorted(POSE_LEVEL_OPTIONAL_TEMPLATES)\n4617\t            if int(self._exact_required_pose_optional_counts.get(tpl, 0)) > 0\n4618\t        ]\n4619\t        required_optional_template_set = set(required_optional_templates)\n4620\t        ordered_groups = self._ordered_groups_for_exact_search()\n4621\t        for group in ordered_groups:\n4622\t            group_id = str(group[\"group_id\"])\n4623\t            ordered_signature_count_vars = [\n4624\t                self._mandatory_signature_count_vars[group_id][str(bucket[\"bucket_id\"])]\n4625\t                for bucket in self._mandatory_signature_buckets.get(group_id, [])\n4626\t                if str(bucket[\"bucket_id\"]) in self._mandatory_signature_count_vars.get(group_id, {})\n4627\t            ]\n4628\t            if ordered_signature_count_vars:\n4629\t                self.model.AddDecisionStrategy(\n4630\t                    ordered_signature_count_vars,\n4631\t                    cp_model.CHOOSE_FIRST,\n4632\t                    cp_model.SELECT_MAX_VALUE,\n4633\t                )\n4634\t            mandatory_signature_counts[group_id] = len(ordered_signature_count_vars)\n4635\t            mandatory_signature_count_literals += len(ordered_signature_count_vars)\n4636\t            candidate_pose_set = {\n4637\t                int(pose_idx) for pose_idx in self._candidate_pose_indices_for_group(group)\n4638\t            }\n4639\t            ordered_pose_indices: List[int] = []\n4640\t            for bucket in self._mandatory_signature_buckets.get(group_id, []):\n4641\t                ordered_pose_indices.extend(\n4642\t                    int(pose_idx)\n4643\t                    for pose_idx in bucket[\"pose_indices\"]\n4644\t                    if int(pose_idx) in candidate_pose_set\n4645\t                )\n4646\t            ordered_vars = [\n4647\t                self.z_vars[group_id][pose_idx]\n4648\t                for pose_idx in ordered_pose_indices\n4649\t                if pose_idx in self.z_vars.get(group_id, {})\n4650\t            ]\n4651\t            if not ordered_vars:\n4652\t                continue\n4653\t            self.model.AddDecisionStrategy(\n4654\t                ordered_vars,\n4655\t                cp_model.CHOOSE_FIRST,\n4656\t                cp_model.SELECT_MAX_VALUE,\n4657\t            )\n4658\t            mandatory_literals += len(ordered_vars)\n4659\t"}]},"uuid":"7c024100-8c51-4fbf-a918-c3b2c2c67723","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:22.308Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a9ccc37e-7458-4f75-bce0-a5718f89093c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c00a5cb3-c770-4c64-a7e1-a5edc5959ad1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDF6cLvJbbtaEtbeKRmi5X","type":"tool_result","content":"I've reviewed the provided content, but the `symmetry_level` proto field and its documented values (0/1/2/3/4) are not present in the portion of the `sat_parameters.proto` file that was displayed.\n\nThe content shown ends at line 286, and the document has 1925 lines total. The `symmetry_level` field appears to be defined later in the file, beyond what was included in the web page excerpt you provided.\n\nTo find this information, you would need to view the complete file or scroll further down in the GitHub repository view."}]},"uuid":"809b6ff3-a766-4f52-bc89-853097957a16","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:23.798Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c00a5cb3-c770-4c64-a7e1-a5edc5959ad1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9ba56f28-3cd0-4302-b1f4-61d8f1d5203c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WnxPGXSmknsDNPGjD7JDgV","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary: Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\n\nBased on the arXiv abstract provided, I can only offer limited information since the full paper content isn't included.\n\n## What the Abstract Reveals\n\nThe paper doesn't propose specific new SBP techniques. Instead, it establishes **theoretical complexity barriers**: the authors prove that \"efficient certification of graph non-isomorphism\" represents a fundamental obstacle to computing complete symmetry breaking predicates efficiently.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n- **Hardness result**: Computing complete SBPs (beyond just lex-leader variants) is fundamentally difficult\n- **Scope limitations**: The complexity barrier applies \"even when SBPs are allowed to introduce additional variables\"\n- **Positive results**: Polynomial-time algorithms exist for specific symmetry groups, including \"automorphism groups of trees and wreath products of groups with efficient SBPs\"\n\n## Practical Implications\n\nThe abstract mentions the results \"explain the difficulty of obtaining short SBPs for important CP problems, such as matrix-models with row-column symmetries and graph generation problems.\" However, **no quantitative pruning results or specific applications to packing problems are detailed** in the abstract.\n\nThe paper was accepted to CP 2024 (Constraint Programming conference), suggesting relevance to constraint solver practitioners, but the abstract doesn't provide concrete empirical data or implementation guidance."}]},"uuid":"11f8f4da-7dc1-44c5-b949-5eb4be69c903","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:26.701Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9ba56f28-3cd0-4302-b1f4-61d8f1d5203c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"efbabf60-5dca-42b6-8a81-d9b197ad0d4d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015XBqfdFTUZ9L558ne6HiYY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"VLSI placement symmetry breaking pairwise lex constraint CP-SAT performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[2407.04419v1] The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.04419v1\"},{\"title\":\"[2407.04419] The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.04419\"},{\"title\":\"General Symmetry Breaking Constraints Toby Walsh1\",\"url\":\"https://cgi.cse.unsw.edu.au/~tw/wcp06.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry in Constraint Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Jean-Francois-Puget/publication/240829678_Symmetry_in_Constraint_Programming/links/00b7d53b6948166dbe000000/Symmetry-in-Constraint-Programming.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding CP-SAT: Constraint Programming Meets SAT Solving | by Jonas Poelmans | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@jonaspoelmans120/understanding-cp-sat-constraint-programming-meets-sat-solving-b137dddb943c\"},{\"title\":\"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON VLSI SYSTEMS, 2004 1\",\"url\":\"https://home.engineering.iastate.edu/~cnchu/pubs/j11.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2407.04419\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry Breaking Ordering Constraints by ZEYNEP KIZILTAN\",\"url\":\"https://www.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:164039/FULLTEXT01.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"custom search constraints and symmetry breaking with cp/sat\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/4tqNoPhFnAw\"},{\"title\":\"The Complexity of Symmetry Breaking Beyond Lex-Leader\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2024.3\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found regarding the topics you queried:\n\n## Symmetry Breaking and Lex-Leader Constraints\n\nSymmetry breaking is a widely popular approach to enhance solvers in constraint programming, such as those for SAT or MIP. Symmetry breaking predicates (SBPs) typically impose an order on variables and single out the lexicographic leader (lex-leader) in each orbit of assignments. Although it is NP-hard to find complete lex-leader SBPs, incomplete lex-leader SBPs are widely used in practice.\n\n## Complexity of Computing Symmetry Breaking Predicates\n\nRecent research has investigated the computational complexity of these approaches. Investigators have looked into the complexity of computing complete SBPs, lex-leader or otherwise, for SAT, with a main result proving a natural barrier for efficiently computing SBPs: efficient certification of graph non-isomorphism.\n\n## Pairwise Constraints and CP-SAT\n\nPairwise constraints are less effective than specialized propagators, as they are far more effective than decomposing into many pairwise constraints. CP-SAT can benefit from symmetry breaking: if the problem has symmetries, adding symmetry-breaking constraints can avoid exploring symmetric variants.\n\n## VLSI Placement Context\n\nWhile the search found materials on VLSI placement constraints in general, the specific integration of symmetry breaking pairwise lexicographic constraints with CP-SAT for VLSI placement performance optimization doesn't appear prominently in the results. The results indicate active research in both symmetry breaking techniques and constraint programming solvers, but a focused study combining all these elements for VLSI applications may be a more specialized or recent topic.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7805e154-55a7-4b47-ae26-a04381c2936e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:30.087Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"efbabf60-5dca-42b6-8a81-d9b197ad0d4d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fcc12b14-b1e0-4d4d-98ae-47165145cb7c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a96266f5aed302928","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KJGs6m8TDicnu62XwdGDqu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"symmetry breaking\" \"identical rectangles\" packing facility layout MILP CP quantitative speedup\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Symmetry-breaking constraints for packing identical rectangles within polyhedra | Optimization Letters | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11590-011-0425-9\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Symmetry-breaking constraints for packing identical rectangles within polyhedra\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257697346_Symmetry-breaking_constraints_for_packing_identical_rectangles_within_polyhedra\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry breaking generalized disjunctive formulation for the strip ...\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/Papers/Strip_pck_Trespalacios.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Hierarchical Rectangle Packing Solved by Multi-Level Recursive Logic-based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2512.20239\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Symmetry-breaking constraints for packing identical rectangles within polyhedra | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Symmetry-breaking-constraints-for-packing-identical-Andrade-Birgin/97864911afaeee9ebe592e6b9cf8561c05834fcd\"},{\"title\":\"Solving the parallel processor scheduling and bin packing problems\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Solving-the-PPSP-and-the-BPP-with-contiguity-constraints-2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Solution Techniques for Two-dimensional Cutting and Packing\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0377221720306111\"},{\"title\":\"Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Journal of Manufacturing Systems\",\"url\":\"https://eprints.sztaki.hu/10802/1/Dobrovoczki_384_35454253_ny.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Model-based Decomposition Algorithms for Complex Routing ...\",\"url\":\"https://tidel.mie.utoronto.ca/pubs/Theses/Zhang_Jiachen_202510_PhD_thesis.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Optimal solution for the 12-rectangle strip-packing problem. | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Optimal-solution-for-the-12-rectangle-strip-packing-problem_fig4_220341822\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found several relevant papers and discussions on symmetry breaking for packing identical rectangles using MILP and CP approaches. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Symmetry-Breaking Constraints for Identical Rectangles Packing\n\nTwo problems related to packing identical rectangles within a polyhedron are addressed through mixed integer programming problems with symmetry-breaking constraints that facilitate the solution of the MIP models.\n\nRectangles are allowed to differ only by horizontal or vertical translations and possibly 90° rotations, with the first problem consisting in packing as many identical rectangles as possible within a given polyhedron, while the second problem consists in finding the smallest polyhedron of a given type that accommodates a fixed number of identical rectangles.\n\n## Facility Layout Applications\n\nThe hierarchical packing problem models core features of packing and placements problems found in design of integrated circuits, design and planning of facility layouts, and packing in logistics.\n\n## MILP vs CP Solver Performance\n\nBoth MILP and CP solvers have been evaluated on single-level packing instances, with the CP solver further utilized within decomposition methods due to its performance.\n\n## Symmetry Breaking in Strip Packing\n\nAn alternative GDP model for the strip packing problem uses additional constraints within the disjunctions in order to break symmetry in the solutions. Results show that MILP reformulations of GDP models can be solved faster than previous approaches.\n\n## Mixed Results on Symmetry Breaking Effectiveness\n\nNotably, CPU times for solving instances without symmetry breaking constraints are sometimes smaller than those displayed when considering the symmetry-breaking constraints, suggesting that symmetry breaking effectiveness depends on problem characteristics.\n\nThe research indicates that while symmetry breaking is theoretically beneficial, the quantitative speedup varies depending on whether the problem is feasible or infeasible, and the complexity of the specific instance being solved.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"677819d5-34d5-4715-914e-8ad8ac208d08","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:29:32.092Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fcc12b14-b1e0-4d4d-98ae-47165145cb7c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
